 "domain": "Number and Algebra",
 "standard_code": "Secondary 1: Equations and Inequalities",
 "standard_url": "https://www.moe.gov.sg/-/media/files/secondary/syllabuses/maths/2020-express_na-maths_syllabuses.ashx",
 "standard_text": "concept of equation; solving linear equations in one variable; solving simple fractional equations that can be reduced to linear equations; formulating a linear equation in one variable to solve problems",
 "meta_desc": "Free Secondary 1 solving equations worksheets: one-step and two-step equations, equations with brackets, negative and fraction answers, and forming equations from word problems. 100 questions with step-by-step answers.",
 "key_big": "Do the same to both sides. Undo + and − first, then × and ÷.",
 "key_sub": "Check your answer by putting it back into the equation.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>An equation is like a balance. Whatever you do to one side, do to the other. Use <b>inverse operations</b> to get the letter on its own: + and − undo each other, × and ÷ undo each other.</p><p>For a two-step equation, undo the + or − first, then the × or ÷.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 4<i>x</i> + 7 = 31.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Subtract 7: 4<i>x</i> = 24.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Divide by 4: <i>x</i> = 6.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 6</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 3(<i>x</i> − 2) = 21.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Divide by 3: <i>x</i> − 2 = 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Add 2: <i>x</i> = 9.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 9</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve <i>n</i>/5 − 3 = 4.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Add 3: <i>n</i>/5 = 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply by 5: <i>n</i> = 35.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>n</i> = 35</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Doing the same operation instead of the inverse: for <i>x</i> + 7 = 20, <i>x</i> = 13, not 27.</li><li>Dividing before subtracting: for 4<i>x</i> + 7 = 31, do 31 − 7 first, then ÷ 4.</li><li>Forgetting that the answer can be negative or a fraction: 4<i>x</i> = 6 gives <i>x</i> = 3/2.</li><li>Not checking: put your answer back into the equation to see if it works.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you solve a two-step equation?",
   "a": "Undo the addition or subtraction first, then the multiplication or division. For 4x + 7 = 31: 4x = 24, so x = 6."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I solve an equation with a bracket?",
   "a": "Either divide both sides by the number outside the bracket, or expand the bracket first. 3(x − 2) = 21 gives x − 2 = 7, so x = 9."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can the answer be a fraction or negative?",
   "a": "Yes. 4x = 6 gives x = 3/2, and x + 9 = 4 gives x = −5."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I check my answer?",
   "a": "Substitute it back. If x = 6 in 4x + 7, then 24 + 7 = 31, so it is correct."
  }
 ],
